<blockquote>
<p>They'd probably just do your standard screening and call it a day.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The standard screening for something like Top Secret Clearance at the NSA would certainly uncover this kind of thing. The Foundation is even more covert than that. If you lie about your background to the NSA and they find out later, you go to Federal prison, but if you lie to the Foundation about your background and they find out later you end up a Jane Doe in an amnesia ward or an unmarked grave.</p>
<p>If she really is schizophrenic, then that really ought to be diagnosable, or she is being treated, either way it seems nigh-unbelievably incompetent for the Foundation to have given her a Level 2 clearance at minimum, exposed her to the paranormal, and not have known.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He's not doubting she was affected by SCP-3054; he's merely doubting that her description of alt-dimension Cragstaff was accurate.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>He <em>lost a researcher</em>, who is probably being held by entities with unknown abilities and motives, and isn't treating this a a severe security breach (which is almost certainly policy). He isn't apparently even willing to take extremely basic measures to confirm her report. He should be fired (and probably terminated) for gross incompetence.</p>
