					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="3122708"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1497314202 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">13 Jun 2017 00:36</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2850687">
									

<p>I've been thinking about this, and I think you might be spot-on. I think I'm going to edit it to remove references to shifting geography, or waking up in a different bed:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The only reasons I added that in was to get across that the building contains way more people than is immediately obvious&#8230; but I think seeing the different faces every day gets that across just as well.</p>
<p>(Also, I want this place to be as non-anomalous as possible. The horror isn't that it shifts around, or that the place is spooky; that makes it seem way more interesting than it is. The horror is its banality &#8212; its sameness &#8212; that this is where you spend the rest of your life. A series of faces you'll never recognize, living in a fog without hope, waiting to die. Enormous stretches of crippling, crushing boredom and tedium punctuated by tiny, acute bursts of terror, paternalism, and cruelty. The shifting geography and waking-up-in-a-new-bed don't really add to that horror, I don't think, and therefore should probably be cut. I can still have her bring up the different faces, which can still be used against her in front of the judge.)</p>
<p>And on a final note: Without that spatial manipulation stuff in the beginning, it makes the moment when she ends right back up at the front doors just a <em>liiiiiittle</em> bit harder to see coming.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
<strong>EDIT:</strong> Yep, made the edit, it was easy to take out &#8212; and I think it improves the story.
<p>Thank you for bringing this up!</p>

